--- THE VERDICT ---
  The control reads 66.86 and 66.82 MHz. CFGMCLK is independently measured at 67.16 MHz
  (the ripple prescaler probe, whose /256 is the textbook period of a binary counter's MSB)
  and at 66.85 MHz on a second run. The control agrees with both.

  => JOHNSON_DIVIDE_RATIO = 256. CONFIRMED.

  => R4/T4 CLOCK = 398.69 MHz and 397.93 MHz -> PUBLISHED VALUE 398.3 MHz (mean of two runs).

THE RATIO ARGUMENT, which does not depend on any divide ratio at all:
  R4/T4 / CFGMCLK = 398.69 / 66.86 = 5.965   and   397.93 / 66.82 = 5.953
  The two probes use the SAME chain, so the ratio of their readings IS the ratio of their
  clocks, whatever that chain's divide ratio is.

CONSEQUENCE, recorded plainly: the AX7203's R4/T4 oscillator measures ~398 MHz, about TWICE the
"200 MHz" recorded in the parent repository's HARDWARE_SSOT.md and docs/fpga/clocking.md. The
measurement is repeatable (two independent runs, two independent control runs, all in one
session) and the ratio argument is independent of the instrument's calibration. What this
portfolio does NOT know is WHY the documentation says 200 MHz: the board may run a ~400 MHz
oscillator, the SSOT's pin assignment may refer to a different board revision, or the documented
value may be a nominal the board does not run at. The measurement stands; the explanation is
recorded as unknown.

